        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The temperature-swing adsorption compressor (TSAC) is a solid-state compressor that has the capability to remove CO2 from a low-pressure source and subsequently store, compress, and deliver at a higher pressure as required by a processor. The TSAC is an ideal interface device for CO2 removal and reduction units in the air revitalization loop of a spacecraft such for oxygen recovery. The concept of TSAC as a potential interface for the carbon dioxide removal and reduction units in a life support system has already been proven and its operation has been demonstrated 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The ultimate objective of the task was to build an Engineering Development Unit (EDU) of an air-cooled TSAC with flight-like components and configuration and to test it in a relevant environment for certifying the technology readiness level of 6 (TRL 6) for this device. This two-bed prototype of TSAC was configured so that it permitted continuous acquisition of CO2 from a low-pressure source such as CDRA and continuous supply of CO2 to the Sabatier reactor at the specified pressure, and was sized to support a four-person crew load on the International Space Station (ISS). The project involved three phases: 1) Development of the thermal model, mechanical design, and model validation (testing) of the air-cooled TSAC, 2) Fabrication and testing of a full-size prototype to process 4kg CO2 per day, and 3) Develop an engineering development unit (EDU) with a technology readiness level (TRL) 6.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Phase I targets were met on schedule on December 15, 2003. Test results indicated that efficiency of the compressor exceeded the design specification by about 50% with less than 30% of the estimated power. Under phase II, a full-size (sized to serve the ISS at a full-crew load) prototype was built and tested in 2004. The validation tests on the compressor were completed successfully on December 31, 2004. After the validation tests, the compressor was reassembled in a shipping crate to prepare for a relevant environment testing at the Space Station Simulation Laboratory at NASA Marshall Space Flight Center. The relevant environment tests on the TSAC were completed successfully in February 2006. The tests were performed in two phases. The Phase I tests were completed in December 2005 and phase II tests were completed in January 2006. Phase I tests included the TSAC performance characterization in integration with the flight-like CDRA. Phase II experiments included integration tests of the TSAC with the CDRA and the Sabatier reactor EDU (Engineering Development Unit). The integrated tests of the TSAC with the CDRA and the Sabatier EDU were the precursor for the third phase of the TSAC EDU development process. 
<p>
        </div>

